WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.660
Steel is the most important material to human
society.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.960
It has been the skeleton of human industry
for centuries, and the advent of the methods

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.590
to mass produce it from iron ore was the impetus
to transform human society from a mostly agricultural

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.060
lifestyle to urban industrialisation.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.450
It forms the backbone of our skyscrapers,
it paves the way for our railways, it shapes

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.820
the engines that powers our society, and the
very tools that forge these objects are made

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:30.840
from steel.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.980
We have spoken before about how the refining
processes of iron determines whether the resulting

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:40.079
material will be steel or iron and how the
exact percentage of carbon present in the

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:43.899
material has dramatic results on the final
material properties, and how the production

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:48.559
methods through time has taken steel from
an expensive material reserved for swords,

00:00:48.559 --> 00:00:53.399
armour and toolmaking, to one that has permeated
into nearly every technology we use in our

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:54.399
lives.

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:58.789
But I failed to tell why that simple addition
of carbon has such a huge effect on the iron,

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:03.030
turning it from a relatively weak material
to one capable of launching an industrial

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.440
revolution, which is what we are going to
learn about today.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.360
Much of our knowledge in crafting steel was
passed down over the centuries from blacksmith

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:15.030
to blacksmith, creating tools for their communities,
so to learn more about this amazing material

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:19.640
and how blacksmiths carefully tailor it’s
material properties, I visited Alec Steele’s

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.220
workshop to create my own knife from scratch.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:28.660
We started our forging process with round
stock 1055 steel with 0.55% carbon content,

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:32.670
placed it in the forge and gradually shaped
it with a power hammer to a rectangular bar

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.670
that could be more precisely shaped into the
shape of our blade using a 3 pound hammer

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.220
that my wee arms struggled to swing after
an hour.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.360
Once we had roughed out the shape we began
to grind and refine our blade.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.790
Eventually producing our blade blank that
would later be grinded to it’s final shape,

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.610
but before that could happen we needed to
perform some metallurgy wizardry through the

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.440
heat treatment process.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.630
To drive home how important this step is,
we tested 4 samples of the same material at

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.470
different stages of the heat treatment process.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.250
This was our sample before the heat treatment
process, which you will quickly see was the

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:05.820
weakest of the bunch.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.740
This is the normalised sample, which was ductile
with a low yield stress.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.989
It took several hammer blows, which it absorbed
through plastic deformation, not ideal characteristics

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:16.940
for a sword or knife.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.660
Next we tested our quench hardened sample,
which is stupid and dangerous and should not

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:21.939
be tried at home.

00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:25.260
This fractured explosively and tore a hole
through Alec’s reflector.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.800
Finally we tested the tempered material, which
absorbed every hammer blow with minimal plastic

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.829
deformation and only broke when we cut a notch
into the material to create a stress concentration

00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:35.829
point.

00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:40.420
This material is tough, capable of absorbing
energy without deforming permanently, and

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.159
hard allowing it to resist damage to the cutting
edge.

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:45.659
It is the ideal material for a blade.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:50.030
If any of these terms confused you, I created
a video called Material Properties 101 that

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.129
you can check out to get a better understanding
of material property terminology.

00:02:54.129 --> 00:02:58.110
So how can the same steel alloy change so
radically by simply applying heat?

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.120
Well this is the magic of the iron carbon
alloy.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.260
We can careful control how the internal metallic
crystal structure forms with heating and cooling

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.360
cycles.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.180
First let’s see how adding carbon to iron
affects it’s crystalline structure.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.739
With no carbon present pure iron will form
a crystal structure called body centred cubic

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:19.379
with an iron atom at each of the eight corners
and another in the centre.

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:23.890
Each crystal structure has a direction it
most easily wants to deform, called a slip

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:24.950
plane.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:28.580
For body centred cubic the slip plane occurs
along this planes.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:32.900
Metals with Body centred cubic crystals like
iron and tungsten tend to be harder and less

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.870
malleable than metals with face centred cubic
crystals like aluminium, lead and gold.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.909
When a metal is cooling, these crystals grow
from individual nucleation points and form

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:46.540
grains where each grain has the same orientation
of slip plane but neighbouring grains may

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.099
not have the same slip plane.

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:51.739
Let’s think about this 2 dimensionally,
when a force is applied, the grain wants to

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:56.400
slip in a particular direction, and passes
the force onto the next grain in that direction

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.260
too, but this grains slip plane is at a different
angle, so that force needs to be greater in

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.760
order to cause deformation.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.989
It’s like trying to push a train down a
railway track, by pushing on its side.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:08.909
It’s not going to go anywhere easily.

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:12.569
So smaller and more numerous grains results
in a stronger material.

00:04:12.569 --> 00:04:17.070
Pure iron tends to always has the same crystal
structure as it cools and it’s crystal structure

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.769
doesn’t change meaningfully with heat treatment.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:21.989
This is where alloying with carbon comes in.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:25.270
To explore this let’s look at our phase
diagram for carbon steel.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.320
On this diagram we have our carbon content
percentage on the x-axis and the temperature

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.250
of the metal on the y-axis.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:34.870
This tells us the crystalline structure of
the metal at various temperatures and carbon

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:35.870
contents.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:40.199
On the left hand side we have pure iron, which
as we explained earlier forms only one crystal

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:41.580
structure, called ferrite.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.461
As we move across the diagram to the right
hand side, less and less of the crystal structure

00:04:45.461 --> 00:04:50.550
forms ferrite, and more forms an iron carbide
alloy, commonly called cementire.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.370
Now if we move up in temperature we start
to see these lines that represent transitional

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:58.330
temperatures, where the crystal structures
of the steel begin to transform into a new

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.830
crystal structure called Austenite.

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:05.460
Moving further up again we see lines representing
the transition of the material to a liquid

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:06.460
state.

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.330
Austenites primary difference to ferrite is
that it forms that face centred cubic crystal

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.840
structure that we saw early, while ferrite
is body centred cubic.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.770
And while this packing pattern is denser than
body centred cubic, it does open up spaces

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.770
in the crystal structure that interstitial
carbon atoms, which are smaller than iron,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.310
can snuggly fit.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:27.150
Allowing austenite to have a higher solubility
to carbon over ferrite.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.919
Using all of this information, let’s take
our 1055 steel with 0.55% carbon content and

00:05:32.919 --> 00:05:37.080
see how it transforms from the start of our
heat treatment cycle to the end.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.259
The first step is called normalisation.

00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:43.300
Normalisation is primarily functions to relieve
internal stresses and strains that formed

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.400
during the forging process and return the
material to its original crystal structure

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.860
before forging began.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:53.540
It’s effectively a reset button for the
steel and creates nice even grain size and

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.460
distribution, increasing its strength.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:59.150
Here we placed the knife blank inside a steel
tube to prevent the metal from receiving heat

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.810
directly from the flame, but instead a more
even radiative heat from the tube.

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:07.151
Once it reaches this transition temperature
we let it soak to give the crystal structure

00:06:07.151 --> 00:06:08.560
time to settle.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.349
The next step in normalising is to allow the
steel to slowly air cool.

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:14.970
What happens now depends on the carbon content.

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:21.370
If we take a 0.8% carbon steel, no hang on
we’re gonna need a bigger graph for this,

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.780
If we take a 0.8% carbon steel and cool it
to it’s through transitional temperature

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:30.120
the austenite and the interstitial carbon
will slowly transform to a mixture of ferrite

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.319
and cementite, which takes this laminar structure
called pearlite.

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:37.870
Pearlite only forms at a 0.8% carbon solution

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.460
Now if we take a 0.55% 0.2% steel, like the
one we used.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.639
And slowly cool it, to its first transitional
temperature here.

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:48.340
Where ferrite first begins to form, ferrite
is pure iron so as it forms the carbon percentage

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.849
begins to rise, this will continue to happen
until the remaining austenite has a carbon

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:57.389
percentage of 0.8% and it will then form pearlite
from this point on.

00:06:57.389 --> 00:07:01.629
This forms a crystal structure dominated by
ferrite, showing here as a lighter colour,

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:03.430
surrounded by the darker pearlite.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:07.710
Comparing these microstructures to another
2 we can see the effect carbon has on the

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.889
microstructure.

00:07:08.889 --> 00:07:13.139
Here we have pure iron, with 100% ferrite,
showing as this light colour, you can even

00:07:13.139 --> 00:07:14.879
see the grain boundaries.

00:07:14.879 --> 00:07:20.110
This is a 0.5% carbon steel similar to ours,
where a very small amount of ferrite formed

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:25.300
before we reaching the point of pearlite formation,
and this is 0.8% carbon steel where the entire

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:30.590
structure is pearlite, with our previous example
showing pearlite under 500 times magnification

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.300
where you can readily see that laminar structure.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.990
How Pearlite strengthens steel is not well
understood.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:40.689
It has little effect on the steels stiffness,
but increasing the pearlite content has dramatic

00:07:40.689 --> 00:07:44.840
effects on the materials yield point, making
it much more capable of absorbing energy without

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.409
permanently deforming.

00:07:46.409 --> 00:07:50.569
But we can increase the materials stiffness
and hardness with our next step.

00:07:50.569 --> 00:07:54.979
If we heat the metal back up to form austenite
once again, but this time instead of letting

00:07:54.979 --> 00:07:59.400
it cool slowly, we rapidly cool it in oil,
while casually burning your arm hair off and

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:04.159
barely flinching, the carbon atoms that spread
out throughout the hot austenite structure

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:08.080
cannot diffuse out of the crystal lattice
to form cementite, and instead gets stuck

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:12.060
in solution, creating a new crystal structure
called martensite.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.100
This crystal structure has a huge amount of
internal stretching.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.810
In part because the carbon trapped within
the crystal structure causes the crystal lattice

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:23.159
to deform, but also because during the rapid
cooling the surface cooled much faster than

00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:24.780
the internal material.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.360
This causes internal tension in the material.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.430
These internal strains make it harder for
additional deformation to occur, but this

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.450
does not make the material stronger.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:37.670
It simply means it will not stretch and bend
before breaking, and when it finally does

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:42.029
fracture all of this internal tension is suddenly
released in an explosive expansion.

00:08:42.029 --> 00:08:45.230
If you have watched Smarter Every Days video
on the Saint Rupert’s Drop, you will have

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:48.839
seen this principle demonstrated in incredible
slow motion.

00:08:48.839 --> 00:08:53.110
This material property is called hardness,
and we want our cutting edge to be hard to

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:57.440
resist damage when cutting, but we do not
want our entire blade to be hard as it will

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.910
not be able to absorb a lot of energy.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.839
It needs to be able to dissipate some of that
energy through heat and deformation.

00:09:03.839 --> 00:09:07.640
This is where the final step of the process
comes in, called tempering.

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:11.730
Tempering raises the temperature enough to
allow the carbon trapped in solution to escape,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:16.400
we just used an oven set at 200 degree, the
carbon then coalesces to form cementite once

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.320
again, but instead of forming pearlite like
before.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.980
It gathers in globules surrounded by ferrite.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:26.320
Tempering also relieves some of that internal
tension caused by the rapid cooling.This reduces

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.740
the hardness, but increases toughness.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:32.560
This produces a material that has the perfect
balance of characteristics between the normalised

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.540
material and the hardened material.

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.339
It is ductile enough to absorb hammer blows
without shattering, but strong enough to not

00:09:38.339 --> 00:09:42.089
permanently deform, and with enough hardness
to ensure it doesn’t gather damage on the

00:09:42.089 --> 00:09:43.290
cutting edge.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:47.420
This process gave our steel the perfect material
properties and a lot of that is thanks to

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:50.360
the quality of the steel we started off with.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:54.060
Learning these skills with Alec was a lot
of fun and I highly recommend you check his

